==English==
[[File:Paulaner.jpg|thumb|[[w:Paulaner Brewery|Paulaner]] dunkel]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|de|dunkel|t=dark}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A type of dark [[German]] [[lager]], typically ranging in color from amber to dark reddish brown, and characterized by its smooth [[malty]] flavor.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A Stomach for Dissent: The Life of Raymond Postgate, 1896-1971|publisher=Keele University Press|year=1994|page=168|passage=I drank '''dunkel''' export beer in the Bier Halle where all the girls were called Therese;}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=The New Brewer|year=1997|page=120|passage=Recently, for instance, he brewed all '''dunkels''' for a period, only with different yeast strains.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Beer Book|publisher=[[w:DK (publisher)|DK]]|year=2008|page=104|passage=Bavarian '''dunkels''' are deep reddish-brown in colour.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gavin D. Smith|title=Beer: A Global History|series=The Edible Series|publisher={{w|Reaktion Books}}|year=2014|isbn=9781780232997|passage='''Dunkels''' tend to be moderately hopped, made using dark malts, and offer malty, toffee, chocolate flavours. While weissbiers, helles and '''dunkels''' are not actually indigenous to Germany, altbier has its origins around the city of Düsseldorf,{{nb...}}.}}

